{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "tamar-veil",
  "text": "This chapter is rough, and it ends with a patriarch admitting a woman he tried to burn was more righteous than him.\n\nGenesis thirty-eight. Tamar marries Judah's oldest son. He dies. By custom she's given to the next brother. He dies too. Judah's got a third son, Shelah, and he tells Tamar go live as a widow at your father's house until he grows up.\n\nAnd then he never sends for her.\n\nShe is parked. No husband, no children, no standing, and no legal way to fight it.\n\nYears pass. She sees Shelah is grown and she has been left there.\n\nSo she takes off her widow's clothes, covers herself with a veil, and sits by the road to Timnath. Judah goes past, doesn't recognise her, and propositions her.\n\nAnd here's where she gets sharp. He offers a young goat. She says, \"Wilt thou give me a pledge, till thou send it?\"\n\nHe says what pledge. And she names three things. His signet, his bracelets, his staff.\n\nThat is his ID. She took his ID.\n\nThree months later, word gets to Judah that she's pregnant. And he says, \"Bring her forth, and let her be burnt.\"\n\nAnd she sends him his own signet and staff with a message. \"Discern, I pray thee, whose are these.\"\n\nAnd Judah says it out loud. \"She hath been more righteous than I.\"",
